# L §8.3 Which refusal prints (tally 2.1.1), with L §6.4: a refusal in a scenario file refuses that scenario and those
# over it. s2 (a_s2.tally) is over s1 (z_s1.tally), and each file's grammar fails on line 5. s1 is refused by its own
# file; s2's reality holds both files, and load parses them in the order of their full paths, so a_s2.tally's refusal
# is the one s2 prints. The base's ask is answered.
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "own"
input x: int

version v
  cite s
  governs 2026..
  known 2026-01-01
  x = 1 "own"

ask q: x law 2026-12-31
=== z_s1.tally
scenario s1 over t
  tally 2
  from 2026-06-01

def y int 1
=== a_s2.tally
scenario s2 over s1
  tally 2
  from 2026-06-01

def w int 2
=== expect
scenario s1: refused: LOAD-PARSE cannot parse: expected ":", got int (z_s1.tally:5)
scenario s2: refused: LOAD-PARSE cannot parse: expected ":", got int (a_s2.tally:5)
q: 1
  law date 2026-12-31, cut 2026-12-31
